  "reconstructibility": 2,
  "reason": "R5 strongly anticipates the central design: adding Cr to a BCC refractory alloy produces a network-distributed Cr2Ta-type Laves phase together with solid-solution and precipitation strengthening. Its WVTaTi matrix is closely similar to the later HfMoTaTiZr refractory system. R1 and R2 further show that dilute solute additions can improve strength without sacrificing—and sometimes while increasing—ductility in closely related Hf-Zr-Ti-Ta-based BCC alloys. The other second-phase records establish effects of phase morphology on strengthening, dislocation motion, and cracking. Thus an expert could plausibly propose dilute Cr alloying of HfMoTaTiZr to generate a networked Cr-rich Laves phase and combine solution and second-phase effects, although the precise 4.2 at.% composition and the specific claim that the brittle network hinders cracks while compensating plasticity are not directly disclosed."
